cff-version: 1.2.0
message: "If you use this work, please cite it as below."
type: article
title: "Coarsening at random for multiple instance learning: identifiability conditions for instance-level inference"
authors:
  - family-names: Towell
    given-names: Alexander
    email: lex@metafunctor.com
    orcid: "https://orcid.org/0000-0001-6443-9897"
    affiliation: "Southern Illinois University Edwardsville"
abstract: >-
  Multiple instance learning (MIL) trains on bags of instances labelled only
  at the bag level: a bag is positive when at least one of its instances is
  positive, and the instance labels are latent. We show that MIL is an
  instance of the masked-data identifiability problem from reliability
  statistics, with instances acting as components, bags as candidate sets,
  the logical-OR bag rule as series aggregation, and singleton bags
  (instance-level labels) as singleton candidate sets that restore
  identifiability. We establish a rank condition for identifiability of
  instance-type positivity and its continuous-feature extension, a
  bag-prevalence consistency theorem, and a first-order bias bound under
  aggregation-rule misspecification. A confound between intrinsic instance
  positivity and the noisy-OR firing rate is identified as the discrete-label
  analogue of the spike-in capture-efficiency gap in scRNA-seq. Simulation
  and an application to the MUSK benchmarks confirm the theory.
keywords:
  - masked data
  - coarsening at random
  - multiple instance learning
  - weak supervision
  - noisy-OR
  - partial-label learning
  - MUSK
  - identifiability
license: CC-BY-4.0
repository-code: "https://github.com/queelius/mil-coarsening"
